  "explanation": [
    "PostgreSQL found the 200 Icelandic customers with a bitmap scan of customers_country_idx (it expected exactly 200), 4 × 200 = 800 here, read all 100,000 orders, joined them in a hash join, 100,000 + 200 = 100,200 plus 1,995 for the result, then sorted the 1,995 rows by customer number, 1,995 × 11 = 21,945: 224,940 in all.",
    "The customers are stored by customer number, so a seq scan returns them in that order, 10,000, and an index nested loop keeps the order of its outer rows. Looking up the Icelanders' 1,995 orders through orders_customer_id_idx costs 4 × (200 + 1,995) = 8,780, and the plan needs no sort: 20,775 in all. The same lookups after customers_country_idx cost 33,520, because that index orders its rows by country, so the result has to be sorted. A nested loop keeps the order of its outer table, so start from one already in the order you need."
